n-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Boschi-Pinto</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><comment>WHO Child Health Epidemiology Reference Group</comment><year>2004</year><article-title>Global estimate of the incidence of clinical pneumonia among children under five years of age</article-title><source>Bull World Health Organ</source><volume>82</volume><fpage>895</fpage><lpage>903</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15654403</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0050241-b024"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Snow</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Eckert</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Teklehaimanot</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Estimating the needs for artesunate-based combination therapy for malaria case management in Africa</article-title><source>Trends Parasitol</source><volume>19</volume><fpage>363</fpage><lpage>369</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12901938</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="n3" fn-type="current-aff"><p>Mike English and J. Anthony G. Scott are with the KEMRI&#x02013;Wellcome Trust Programme, Centre for Geographic Medicine Research&#x02013;Coast, in Kilifi and Nairobi, Kenya. Mike English is also with the Department of Paediatrics and J. Anthony G. Scott is with the Nuffield Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, John Radcliffe Hospital, Oxford, United Kingdom.</p></fn><fn id="n1" fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding:</bold>Mike English (&#x00023;076827) and J. Anthony G. Scott (&#x00023;081835) are supported by Wellcome Trust Senior Research Fellowships for their work in Kenya. The Wellcome Trust played no part in the preparation of this manuscript or the decision to submit it for publication.</p></fn><fn id="n2" fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests:</bold> Mike English and J. Anthony G. Scott have at times provided advice to WHO panels on common childhood illnesses and their management in low-income countries. There are no other conflicts of interest.</p></fn><fn id="ack1" fn-type="con"><p><bold>Author contributions.</bold> This article is intended to promote debate on the future of guidelines that aim to guide treatment for hundreds of millions of children each year. To this end Mike English conceived the idea for the manuscript, reviewed relevant literature, identified those recent references that best illustrate the issues, and prepared a first draft of the manuscript. Both authors worked to produce the final version. Both Mike English and J. Anthony G. Scott have worked for more than a decade in Kenya within the general field of international child health. Both authors approved the final version of the manuscript.</p></fn><fn><p><bold>Provenance:</bold> Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>